  "text": "ству\n.Sprintf you, let's break down the problem step by step to understand the expected number of regions into which the 27 line segments divide the disk.\n\n1. **Initial Division of the Disk**:\n   - The disk is divided into four quadrants by two perpendicular diameters intersecting at the center. This initial division creates 4 regions.\n\n2. **Adding More Line Segments**:\n   - Alex adds 25 more line segments, each drawn by selecting two points at random on the perimeter of the disk in different quadrants and connecting those two points.\n\n3. **Expected Number of Regions**:\n   - To find the expected number of regions, we need to consider how each new line segment intersects with the existing regions. Each new line segment can potentially intersect with all the existing regions, and each intersection can create additional regions.\n\n4. **Using the Concept of Expected Value**:\n   - The expected number of regions \\( R \\) after adding \\( n \\) line segments can be approximated using the formula:\n     \\[\n     R \\approx 1 + \\frac{n}{2} + \\frac{n(n-1)}{4}\n     \\]\n   - Here, \\( n = 27 \\).\n\n5. **Calculating the Expected Number of Regions**:\n   - Plugging in \\( n = 27 \\) into the formula:\n     \\[\n     R \\approx 1 + \\frac{27}{2} + \\frac{27 \\times 26}{4}\n     \\]\n   - Simplifying the terms:\n     \\[\n     R \\approx 1 + 13.5 + \\frac{702}{4}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     R \\approx 1 + 13.5 + 175.5\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     R \\approx 190\n     \\]\n\nTherefore, the expected number of regions into which the 27 line segments divide the disk is approximately 190. The final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{190}\n\\]",
